――I loved the sound of something cracking and breaking.

Thin ice formed over a puddle on a morning when breath turned white.

An old, yellowed plate holding a meager meal.

The many windows glittering across my hometown's streets, visible all at once from the hill.

I would pick up a shard of ice, a fragment of pottery, a piece of windowpane, and rub my fingertips together.

With a faint, raspy sound, the fragment would shatter to dust in my hand, and as if chasing after it, every matching thing in my sight would crack and break all at once.

Just like a sandcastle collapsing, the end would spread from one edge to the other.

I wanted to hear more sounds, bigger things breaking, more and more of it.

My hometown was famous as a quarrying town, and there was a white stone used in every house and every building. I secretly took a piece of it and climbed to the highest point.

On that hilltop overlooking my hometown in every direction, I raised my hand with a trembling chest and ground the small piece of white stone between my fingertips.

――The sound of everything cracking, everything breaking, made my whole body tremble with delight.

Annoying voices, hateful ties to people, irritating dealings with others—all of it was drowned out by that sound, unable to reach my ears.

Watching something so grand—something someone had struggled and devoted themselves to build for whatever reason—crack, break, and crumble away was irresistible.

So I craved it, wandering on and on, wanting more, and more, and more.

I abandoned my hometown, now a mountain of rubble and debris, and went on creating mountains of rubble and debris here and there, searching for the next place fit to become one—walking, walking, walking.

Cracking and breaking. Cracking and breaking again. Cracking, over and over.

And so, wanting to crack and break something even bigger, something even prettier, I pointed my finger at that old, grand castle so worth cracking――

"――My, my, aren't you a bad child, trying to cause mischief? That's my castle, you know, and here's an ill-mannered girl. Looks like you're worth disciplining, doesn't it?"

At that voice, I heard the sound of something cracking and breaking, terribly close by.

Why was that? I had loved the sound of things cracking and breaking so much—yet that one sound, which still kept ringing endlessly, made me feel sick, so sick I couldn't stand it.

I wanted to drown it out, so I couldn't help but crack and break something else again.

"――I broke it. ...I'm sure I broke it."

Eyes wide in shock, Clarissa stared blankly at the sight before her.

The long Milesta Dragonback Bridge tilted heavily, warped, its collapse only a matter of time. Facing that impending end, the pandemonium grew, and everyone kept making the wrong choices.

The voices of people who gave up trying to stave off the end, crying and begging for forgiveness, sounded like unimaginably loathsome noise to Clarissa's ears. ――Why did people insist on staying near big, beautiful, grand things?

Because of that hateful human habit, impurities mixed into the moving sound of big, beautiful, grand things cracking and breaking—the sound Clarissa yearned for.

Humans could stay dead forever, except when they were making something worth cracking and breaking. She wished they would only crawl up when making those grand, beautiful things, swarm together to finish them, and then die again.

She wished they would forever do nothing but build things for Clarissa to break, and when they were broken, build something new from the mountain of rubble and debris.

Breaking formless things didn't quench the thirst in Clarissa's heart.

That was why, when she heard she could break something big and grand, she had followed Dandon's orders too. Coming all the way out to a place like this, even though it wasn't Mother's command.

All of it, every bit of it, was because she'd been told she could crack and break this bridge.

"――!"

Clenching her back teeth, Clarissa ignored the tilted footing and rushed to the crack in the road—the wound that had made the bridge scream—and saw the loathsome glow filling it.

Glittering in deep violet, it was the trace of the magic arrows fired from the castle that had suddenly appeared. The arrows had dyed the spots they hit deep violet, patching up the wounds across the bridge like this.

Like the ice just moments ago, it was a makeshift treatment holding the breaking bridge together, but it had one decisive difference from the ice. ――It was something Clarissa had never shattered before.

Whether stone, glass, or iron, she could grind it to dust with the fingertips of this gauntlet.

Yet this deep violet crystal—this thing made of magic—rejected Clarissa's fingertips and refused to shatter.

Therefore――

"I can't... break it...!"

Just a little more, just a tiny bit more, yet at this rate, she couldn't break the bridge. ――Unable to drown out the sound of her own soul creaking and cracking inside her head.

With the dizzying shifts in the situation, everyone's heads were filled with question marks.

In the midst of an imminent crisis, wave after wave of sudden change easily swept people's minds away. ――In that torrent of information, the first to move was none other than Felt.

The moment she judged that the crumbling bridge had been reinforced by support from the suddenly appeared castle and the sinking tilt of the footing had stopped, Felt spun around and searched for the enemy.

There was no need to sweep her gaze around, however. Far from running, the enemy was kneeling right in front of a crack at her feet—one filled with magical light—frantically scratching at the ground with her fingers.

Frankly, it made no sense what she was doing, but――

"I ain't lettin' you keep doin' whatever the hell that is!"

Even if she couldn't grasp the intent, nothing good would come of letting the enemy have her way.

Looking closely, the green-haired woman's cheeks were twitching as she desperately clawed at the road. If that was resistance against having her plans thwarted, then her actions had a purpose.

To stop her from achieving it, Felt kicked off the ground and raised her dagger again—the next instant, she heard the sound of something slicing the air behind her head and instinctively leaned her upper body aside.

"――?!"

Grazing Felt's tilted cheek and neck, a sharply pointed impact pierced into the railing. Eyes wide at the resounding crash and the force that shook the entire bridge, Felt turned to see what had caused it, only to doubt her eyes even further.

There stood――

"...A giant... spider?"

Felt muttered in disbelief. Blocking her view was a black spider of monstrous size and repulsive appearance—a giant spider as large as a dragon carriage.

"――――"

Eight legs thicker than a human torso, covered in jet-black bristles as hard as iron. Every time its joints bent and extended, its carapace gleamed dully with a wet sheen, and inside its opening and closing mandibles, countless dagger-like fangs clicked together.

It was horribly huge, but up to that point, its features matched the spiders Felt knew.

Yet one single feature—just one—denied all of that. ――Its eyes.

In the center of its round head, above the mandibles brimming with drooling, ferocious fangs, sat two eyes. Felt had never studied spiders closely, but she knew that what belonged there were pitch-black, cluster-like eyeballs.

Yet what sat there were not black clusters, but human eyes. Two human eyeballs.

"That ain't a Witchbeast...!"

With a monstrosity this large, the first thought that crossed her mind was an unknown Witchbeast, but that one feature—the eyes—ruled out the likeliest possibility.

It was an aberration born of malice, crafted by a different logic than Witchbeasts――

"What the hell do you think humans are...!"

Sensing what had brought about that grotesque transformation, Felt trembled with anger. Before her, the giant spider raised its front legs and straddled the path, blocking the way to Clarissa. With that movement, a stranded dragon carriage was violently swept aside, and immediately, the shriek of a ground dragon too slow to flee rang out――

"Shit!"

As if on cue, the charging giant spider's piercing strikes lunged at Felt.

Raising its leg tips and slamming them down. It was a primitive act, nothing more than repetition, yet each blow was sharp and lethal enough to punch holes into the stone-paved road. Given the size difference, blocking or parrying wasn't an option for Felt.

Naturally, she had no choice but to leap backward, again and again, fleeing the advancing giant spider.

"Get back! The bridge won't break now! Help each other and run, run, run!"

Somehow dodging the giant spider's relentless attacks, Felt shouted at the top of her lungs.

She had no room to look around, so the evacuation on the bridge was beyond her awareness. Still, the giant spider's two eyes were fixed on Felt, and it didn't seem to have any attention to spare elsewhere either.

Taking advantage of that, Felt poured every nerve into dodging. ――Her eyes couldn't keep up. Surrendering herself to the tingling sensation prickling her skin, she leaped, twisted, leaned, slid, and dodged.

Dodged, dodged, dodged, dodged, dodged, dodged, dodged, dodged――!

"At this rate――"

"Gyahun?!"

Just as she thought she could do it, letting her heated mind push past her limits――

She collided with someone standing behind her, tangled up, and tumbled over. Wondering what soft sensation she was sandwiched in, she saw a large chest ill-suited to a fluttering nun's habit――

"Filore! Don't get in my way!"

"I'm not getting in the way! I was just returning a crying boy to his mother, and... wh-a spider?! And it's huge?! I-I-I've been terrified of spiders since I was little!!"

"What a coincidence! I just started hatin' 'em a second ago myself!"

Still clutching Felt's head to her chest from the tumble, Filore had her pinned. While Felt struggled to break free, the giant spider aimed two legs at their heads. ――In that instant, Felt sensed the spider's attention, which had been fixated on her until a moment ago, split in two.

She didn't know if it was simply because there were now two prey within reach, or because of the faint hatred that flickered in those human-like, emotion-filled eyes.

"――! I am Felt's older sister――!!"

Not knowing which it was, Filore twisted her body and bore Felt down beneath her, trying to shield her from the falling leg tips.

She was a foolish sister who ignored the fact that if someone got hurt, Filore—who could heal with the power of the Sacrament—surviving gave them both a better chance to live.

Felt tried to shove her away, to at least reduce the wounds they'd both take, but――

"I won't make――"

"――Apologies for being late."

In that instant, just before the two descending legs could pierce Felt's and Filore's chests and torsos, arms shot out, grabbed them, and held them fast in midair.

Before their wide eyes, the muscles in the thick arms gripping the spider's legs bulged, and immediately after, the six legs planted on the road were lifted off the ground, and the giant spider was hurled over the railing.

"――――!!"

Letting out a grating screech like nails scratching glass, the spinning giant spider vanished off the bridge. Watching it go in stunned silence, Felt slowly turned her gaze back.

Her eyes met those of the man looking right at them――

"L-L-L-L-Lord Marcos!! We're saved! We barely made it out alive!"

"So it would seem. I am glad I made it in time. ――Are you unharmed as well, Lady Felt?"

"Barely. A second later, and the debate over whether me or Filore is the real one would've been settled for good."

"Stop talking like that! We were both saved! Can't we just both be the real one?!"

They obviously couldn't, but she understood why Filore wanted to say that, so she let it slide.

Taking the hand of Filore, who had gotten up first, Felt stood and looked up at Marcos's massive frame. "So," she said,

"Thanks for the save, but should you really be worryin' about us? What about the dragon carriages about to fall..."

"As for that, he has it handled."

"He? ...Wait, what the hell is that?"

Looking down the bridge where Marcos pointed, Felt grimaced deeply. Ahead, a large merchant caravan had been in particularly dire straits with its wheels trapped in the broken bridge's cracks—yet the ground dragons and dragon carriages forming its convoy were being effortlessly lifted by human hands and carried out.

Unless everyone had suddenly gained superhuman strength, the trick likely lay with the suspicious figure in a mask and black robes shouting at them as if kicking their rears into gear.

"Kuhahaha! Come on, come on, hurry, run, sprint! To think the magic I swiped from the pint-sized 'Librarian' would come in handy—looks like you lot have been surprisingly good in your daily lives!"

Despite his high-handed tone, the suspicious figure was consistently throwing himself into helping people.

His attitude made her a little uneasy, but appearing at a time like this, there was no doubt he was connected to the castle carrying Subaru and the others. ――Or rather, he was probably one of Subaru's people.

"Feels like Big Bro's always savin' me in the weirdest ways..."

Come to think of it, it had been like that ever since the very first loot house. Back then too, Felt's life had been targeted by the 'Bowel Hunter' who hired her, and Subaru had saved her at the last moment.

Emilia had been there too. ――Old Man Rom and Reinhard weren't here.

"...No time to be makin' a serious face. Rock-face! It's that woman over there!"

"Yes, I am aware. However――"

Felt pointed past the overturned dragon carriage toward the culprit woman glimpsed beyond it, but Marcos's reply lacked conviction. The reason was immediately clear. ――The giant body he had thrown off had landed back on the bridge.

"Eek, the giant spider...!"

"Tch! Just like a spider, droppin' a thread to climb back up..."

"――Both of you, step back. I will handle that."

Shielding the cowering Filore at an angle, Felt glared at the returned giant spider. Stepping in front of the two, Marcos held them back with an open palm.

Seeing that outstretched palm, however, Felt gasped, "Rock-face...!" Marcos's palm was burned raw, oozing blood.

"How'd that happen... don't tell me it was that bastard's legs?! Poison?!"

"If one touches it carelessly, yes. Judging by the melting road, it is a strong acid. I could not live it down if something happened to the young ladies' skin."

"Is this the time to be sayin' that?! If you're not careful, your whole body's gonna melt――"

"――Ah, forgive my polite phrasing. You're in the way. Get back, brats."

"L-Lord Marcos?"

Taken aback by his unexpectedly rough tone, Felt stiffened, and beside her, Filore's cheek twitched. Before the two of them, Marcos clenched his painfully wounded fist—and with a slow grinding sound, his hand, arm, and shoulder began to be covered in rock.

And then――

"If you're so proud of a body you can't touch, stepping in front of me is the height of stupidity. I've been pent up with all these idiots I can't punch. I'm going to beat you to a pulp."

As he spoke, Marcos's body was encased in rock armor along with his fierce fighting spirit. Watching his already towering frame grow a size or two larger, Felt realized there was no point staying there and grabbed Filore's hand.

Pulling the bewildered girl's arm as she yelped "Ueh?!", Felt ran further down the bridge.

"Rock-face! That big bastard's all yours! We're goin' this way!"

"B-But, Felt! Lord Marcos! The spider's poison is turning his body to stone!"

"Rock-face is doin' that to himself!"

"Himself?! Why?!"

Glancing back while being pulled along, Filore asked. Felt's eyes widened at the question, then she flashed a small grin showing her canine tooth. "Isn't it obvious?"

""――Because it's easier to punch that way.""

Felt's mutter overlapped with the muffled voice inside the rock helmet as a heavy step was taken. The next instant, the giant spider's countless piercing strikes rained down on Marcos—yet all of them were deflected by the sturdy rock armor, neither impact nor poison able to reach inside 'the Boulder'.

"Telling you to die is a bit crude. ――Drop dead!"

As if in return, a massive rock fist slammed straight into the giant spider's face.

――When an unfamiliar castle rose into the sky beyond the bridge, breaking through the river mist, everyone around her was stunned, but Emilia's eyes alone were drawn to a single spot on the balcony.

Of course, Emilia was very surprised that a castle had popped out too. She had never imagined a castle could fly, and she didn't know why it was flying. Besides, she had been desperately trying to hold up the bridge about to fall, only for the ice meant to anchor it to be shattered, leaving her head spinning in a hopeless situation.

But surely, that was precisely why, Emilia thought.

Always, every single time Emilia was in huge trouble and facing a 'big pinch', the one who came to save her at the crucial moment was her precious knight.

So even when the flying castle appeared, she was surprised, yet immediately knew it was Subaru's doing. Once she knew that, all she had to do was look for the show-off Subaru. ――That was why Emilia found Natsuki Subaru faster than anyone else while everyone was gawking at the flying castle.

The surprise at the castle flying came afterward, with a "Whoa, how?"

"――Honestly, you really always surprise me."

At her knight's antics, Emilia's lips involuntarily curved into a smile.

Beatrice's Yin magic, raining down from the castle balcony, crystallized spots across the Milesta Dragonback Bridge just before it could fall, forcibly holding it in place.

The principle of the Yin magic Minya was very scary—warping the passage of time on whatever it hit, making it brittle and shattering it. However, Beatrice was a Great Spirit of the Yin attribute—in her hands, the magic's effect could be converted to purposes other than shattering.

That was how the Dragonback Bridge, which Emilia alone couldn't protect, had been saved.

"If Subaru and the others are here, the bridge will be fine. Now..."

Extending a hand to those still calling for help and getting everyone across the bridge was the top priority. Reuniting with Subaru and Beatrice was terribly agonizing to put off, but it had to wait until after.

Therefore――

"Stop getting in the way! At this rate, you might fall too!"

"――Yes, right! Right! I was thinking the exact same thing! You really are in the way! You should just give up right now instead of doing this! You half-devil!"

"You shouldn't say mean things like that to someone you just met!"

Saying so, Emilia formed twin ice swords in both hands and lunged at the opponent blocking her—a blonde girl who had suddenly appeared and cut off her path.

The girl with a lovely face and blonde hair trimmed at the shoulders widened her round eyes at Emilia's scolding—and smiled brightly.

And then――

"Uh-huh, I get it! Bad-mouthing is an absolute no! Especially to someone you just met—someone that rude is better off dead. I completely agree!"

"――! You...!"

At the sword strike the girl swung while smiling, Emilia couldn't help but widen her eyes.

Not because the petite girl swung a sword, but because of the swords themselves—gripped in both her hands were twin swords made of ice, just like Emilia's.

And not just similar. The blade length, the 'design' of the hilt and grip, were almost completely identical to the ice swords Emilia made――

"You're copying me?!"

"Yes, copying! No, wait, I was just thinking the exact same thing as you! But if you think I'm copying, I think that's fine too! Everything is just as you say!"

"Huh? Huh? Which is it, then? Copying? Or the same special skill?"

"Right, I think so too! I wish you'd make it clear which one it is! Jeez, which one do you prefer? Decide which one you want, you indecisive thing!"

Exchanging sword strikes with the laughing girl, Emilia felt her head spin at the way she talked.

Did she have no intention of talking properly, or did she actually want to?

"I'm Emilia! Who are you?!"

"Right! Greetings are super important! I was thinking the same thing! I'm Luciereca!"

"Thank you! Now stop getting in the way!"

"Yes, gratitude is super important! And people in the way are super bothersome! I think so too!"

Pushed back by the sword wind of the girl who called herself Luciereca, Emilia struggled.

Even though she kept saying they felt the same, Emilia didn't feel like they were on the same page at all. Moreover, Luciereca's movements felt unpredictable—unlike normal swordsmanship, almost animal-like, making her very hard to fight.

Creating ice weapons in midair, she wove magic and kicks into her swordplay. Ice stakes, ice blades, ice footholds—it was wildly erratic and all over the place.

"Felt-chan and Filore are...!"

The carriages carrying everyone else were further ahead than Emilia, who had been the first to jump off.

Earlier, Felt seemed to have found the person who had broken the bridge. If she was holding that person down, Emilia could do something, but――

"If I fill the bumps in the road with ice, we could push the stuck dragon carriages out..."

If she poured cold air into the cracks and built ice from there, she could avoid freezing the wheels in place. Thinking that, she tried to spread ice whenever Luciereca left an opening, yet no matter how much Emilia tried to freeze the ground,

"I'll break it. I broke it!"

Sticking her tongue out playfully, Luciereca clacked her twin swords together in front of her chest. Instantly, cracks ran through both blades, shattering them to dust with a light sound—and right after, the ice Emilia had spread on the ground and the ice swords in her hands shattered all at once.

"This is the same thing that broke the ice on the bridge...!"

"Yes, it is! It's so surprising, isn't it? I never thought it'd be this fragile!"

Emilia gasped as Luciereca slid in front of her and delivered a kick, sending Emilia flying backward despite crossing her arms in time.

She wanted to get past her and move forward, yet she'd been forced back again. Clenching her back teeth, Emilia watched as Luciereca threw both hands toward the sky with a "Hup!"

Just like that――

"Uh-huh, I get it! We don't have time to be doing this, right? I think you're completely right! So let's end it! There, all done!"

Above Luciereca's head as she laughed and declared that—meaning high above the Milesta Dragonback Bridge—an absurdly massive block of ice had been formed.

"――――"

Seeing it, Emilia was left speechless.

That huge mass of ice, as big as a small mountain, rivaled the one Emilia had once made at Roswaal's request for the battle to retake the imperial capital of the Vollachia Empire.

If something like that fell, no matter how hard Beatrice tried, the bridge would be destroyed this time. And so many people would be crushed by it――

"No! Stop it, Luciereca!"

"Yes, right! Just like you say, I think I should stop doing this too! Yet I keep going anyway—aren't people who don't listen the worst?! Awful!"

Nodding along to Emilia's plea while not listening at all, Luciereca swung her arm down. Following her arm's motion, the mountain-sized block of ice plummeted straight down from the sky.

Instinctively, Emilia fired magic to stop it, but the ice bullets she hadn't had enough time to charge could only scratch the mountain, not break it.

What could she do――

"――Emilia-tan!!"

"――――"

"We'll handle it! Trust us!!"

At the voice thrown from the castle far above, Emilia's heart leaped.

Stirred by a feeling almost sharp enough to be mistaken for pain, Emilia met the eyes of Subaru and the others, who remained on the flying castle's balcony, firing Yin magic at the damaged bridge.

The moment she saw his nod, hesitation vanished from within Emilia, and her step accelerated.

"Whoa, ignoring me! You're ignoring me?! I thought that was best too! Who cares what happens to anyone else, right?! I totally get it!"

"No, if you think that, then you don't understand me at all."

"Yes, I think so too! I don't get you at all! You crazy woman!"

"That's wrong too! I'm not like that—I'm a troublesome woman!!"

As the shadow of the falling iceberg slowly closed in from above, Emilia kicked off the tilted footing with force, closing the distance to Luciereca in one go.

Raising both hands, she formed a huge, huge, absurdly huge ice hammer. As she moved to slam it down, Luciereca's eyes sparkled,

"That's it! I thought that was good too!"

"Really? I thought this was reeally heavy and impossible!"

"Huh?"

Saying that, Emilia abruptly let go, dropping the too-heavy-to-swing ice hammer on the spot. And Luciereca, who had copied Emilia and made an ice hammer of the exact same size, couldn't lift hers either and stood frozen in place.

Right then――

"Hi-yaaah――!!"

Twisting in midair, Emilia drove a back kick straight into Luciereca's torso.

The direct hit folded Luciereca's body in half, sending her flying backward with a "Kyaah!" yelp. The girl's body bounced across the bridge like a ball and slammed into the side of an overturned dragon carriage, coming to a stop. Emilia pinned Luciereca to the carriage with ice, then gasped and frantically looked up at the iceberg looming overhead.

Its base was already inches away from crashing into the Milesta Dragonback Bridge――

"――Ul!"

"Murak, I suppose――!!"

Immediately after, the chant heard from beyond the iceberg filled the entire mass of ice with a pale light. What it did wasn't shatter the iceberg.

Instead, a single shot arriving a beat after the chant caused a small explosion on the iceberg's flank. ――It was a fireball fired by a slender figure standing on the same castle balcony as Subaru and the others.

If she remembered right, he was one of Felt's camp—that realization was blown clean out of Emilia's head by her astonishment at the next sight.

"Whoa."

Before Emilia's wide eyes, the iceberg that had been casting a hopeless shadow over the Milesta Dragonback Bridge bounced away like a scrap of paper from that single small explosion on its side.

Spinning sideways at tremendous speed, the block of ice sailed right over the Dragonback Bridge. The moment it landed in the forest stretching beyond the gorge, the small mountain reclaimed its true weight, slamming the earth with a heavy roar and violent tremor.

"I see. They made it light, and then..."

Clasping her hands in front of her chest, Emilia accurately read what had just happened.

Subaru and Beatrice had erased the iceberg's weight with Yin magic, and while it was light, had an ally hit it to knock it off course. That was far easier said than done—it required evenly applying magic to an iceberg of that size and timing a brief duration precisely for their ally. Truly an amazing feat worthy of a Great Spirit.

Now――

"――!"

At the edge of Emilia's vision as she started to turn, a loud shattering sound echoed. Looking over, the iceberg that had crushed the forest was cracking apart, turning into scattered shards of ice.

And if the ice had broken, that meant――

"...I broke it."

Having shattered her ice restraints, Luciereca stood atop the dragon carriage and stuck out her tongue. At that attitude—equal parts composure and sour grapes—Emilia fixed her gaze on her. "Luciereca!"

"There's no point keeping this up! Even if you try to drop another big block of ice, my knight will handle it. And next time, I won't stop at just one kick!"

"Right! Doing something that failed once over and over is pointless because it'll just fail the same way. I get it, I get it. I really, really get it! And because I get it... there, all done!"

"...All done?"

"Yes, all done for today! You're sick of looking at my face by now, aren't you? I think so too! I'm already tired of looking at yours! So I'm going home! Come on!"

Saying something utterly selfish, Luciereca clapped her hands twice. Emilia hurriedly braced herself against the unpredictable girl, but the movement didn't come from her. ――It came crashing down fiercely from behind Luciereca, from the far side of the bridge.

It was a massive giant spider, moving on eight thick, long, jet-black legs. Emilia tensed for a moment at the appearance of the giant spider covered in steel-like bristles, but――

"...It's reeally beat up?"

"Whoa! You're right! It's reeally beat up! Beat up? More like half-dead! How are you even still alive like that?! Why don't you pick a side? Like just dying already!"

"――――!!"

The giant spider that had leaped in was terribly battered, its steel-like bristles notwithstanding, subjected to tremendous violence and bleeding from all over. And despite having somehow rushed over in that state, it was being berated horribly by its own ally, Luciereca.

Even Emilia hesitated to defend the giant spider, though――

"The orders were to let it die or bring it back, but both look kinda tough! Still, that's the result of your hard work, so it's not my fault, right?! Good job!"

Saying that, Luciereca swiftly leaped onto the giant spider's back. At the sudden shift, Emilia instinctively reached out, "Wait!"

From atop the giant spider, Luciereca smiled at Emilia like a blooming flower,

"Sure! Why, not!"

"――!"

In an instant, the giant spider's massive body vanished from before her eyes, and Emilia took a sharp blow to her side from a presence that appeared right beside her, sending her flying violently.

She slid across the road and stopped against the railing. Looking up, she saw it was a strike from the giant spider, which had leaped the short distance in the blink of an eye; its raised black leg glistened slickly. Liquid dripped from its tip, sizzling on the road and sending up white steam.

Poison, or acid. A direct hit would burn a hole right through her clothes and into her body. ――If it had been a direct hit.

"Right! That's the right answer! If you're protecting yourself in a split second, surrounding yourself with ice is best! I thought that was best too!"

Emilia had reflexively blocked the spider's leg with a thick ice shield, but the impact and being blown back kept her from chasing Luciereca atop the spider. She tried to summon ice stakes in midair to pursue, but every single one shattered before it could fire.

So――

"――That's enough for now! We're being told to come back, and I think that's best too! So stop dragging your feet and stand up, Clarissa! You slowpoke!"

The giant spider reached the culprit who had tried to collapse the bridge—Clarissa—in a single bound, and Luciereca called out to her.

She didn't want to let Luciereca and the giant spider escape either. But even more than them, Clarissa—who could collapse something as huge as the Milesta Dragonback Bridge—could not be allowed to get away.

Trying to stop them from taking Clarissa away, Emilia braced herself to stand, but the painful blow to her side kept strength from returning to her knees.

At this rate――

"――Clarissa?"

Luciereca's puzzled voice reached Emilia's ears. At Luciereca's distracted reaction, Emilia raised her head to see what was happening.

――Clarissa's gauntleted hand was holding a deep violet crystal between her fingertips.

The noise around her didn't reach Clarissa's ears at all.

Tremors and roars, screams and impacts—they flew over her head or crawled across the ground, desperately trying to tip the scales holding the fate of the Milesta Dragonback Bridge.

She could sense it. ――No, that was a lie. Even that was outside Clarissa's world.

In Clarissa's world, she only needed fragile, beautiful things, and the sound of breaking them.

She didn't need humans, or things not worth breaking. And above all, beautiful things that couldn't be broken were unacceptable. That was why she couldn't tolerate this transformed bridge.

The deep violet gleam holding the cracked, crumbling Dragonback Bridge together was beautiful.

Enough to make Clarissa's heart tremble and groan. Precisely because of that, not being able to break it with her own hands was unbearable to Clarissa.

For that, Clarissa panted, sweat beading on her forehead, and frantically clawed at the glow. She knew that something capable of anchoring a falling bridge was on a completely different level of sturdiness from stone or steel.

Still, she clawed desperately, desperately, desperately, and thinking that if it was a magical glow she should pit magic against magic, she demanded a magic blade from Luciereca.

Just one blade. If only she had a single knife imbued with magic.

"Sure, fine! I was just wanting to give you a present anyway! If you want something like this, you can have it! Take it, you penny-pincher!"

The ice dagger thrown to her along with the insult had shattered while she used it. Yet Clarissa used its fragments to desperately scrape at the deep violet glow. Her nails tore off, her hands became covered in blood, and finally, just a tiny chip broke loose, and then――

"――That's enough for now! We're being told to come back, and I think that's best too! So stop dragging your feet and stand up, Clarissa! You slowpoke!"

The noise came again.

The noise was trying to take Clarissa back. What a joke. The power of 'Shatterer' was heavily influenced by Clarissa's line of sight and distance. Even from afar, she might be able to crack the crystallized bridge and bring it down with the shock. ――To hell with that.

"I refuse. ...I refuse."

"――Clarissa?"

"Go back alone... please go back. I have something to do... I'll do it."

Luciereca, riding on the back of the ugly monster, widened her round eyes even further at that. However, she immediately showed her usual radiant smile, like the happiest girl in the world,

"Right! I think that's great! I always thought being all alone suited you! Nobody likes you anyway! You gloomy, spiteful woman!"

Hurling those insults, Luciereca slapped the giant spider's back. At that, the giant spider vaulted over the railing, leaped off the bridge, and swung away on a thread shot onto the gorge wall.

Chasing after the giant spider, an empty dragon carriage bed flew like a cannonball with ferocious force. It didn't reach the spider, however, shot down by an ice bullet fired by Luciereca. The one who had thrown the carriage bed was an ugly giant clad head to toe in rock armor.

"Nobody, come near. Stay away. Please."

Those who had been fighting Luciereca and the giant spider, those scrambling to stop Clarissa, those desperately swept up in the chaos—all of them were annoying.

Right now, she wanted to break something beautiful. Only that sound could save Clarissa. ――The hideous sound cornering her, with nowhere to run, was ringing inside her.

"This――"

The deep violet crystal she had picked up was pinched between the claws of Clarissa's gauntlet. If she rubbed her fingertips together and crushed the crystal, the destruction would spread, and the Milesta Dragonback Bridge would fall.

She didn't need a plan to escape right now. Right now, she just wanted to drown out this noise――

"――?"

To stop the noise, she tried to rub her fingers together, and couldn't. For some reason, the fingers trying to do it wouldn't move. ――No, not just her fingers, her arm, her shoulder, her whole body. Worse, the glow of the crystal pinched between her fingers was moving away from Clarissa.

She couldn't stop the crystal from tilting and drifting away. She couldn't stop it, yet she felt no panic. At that moment, Clarissa's mind was captivated by a faint sound she had heard.

It was different from the sound of cracking and shattering beautiful things with the power of 'Shatterer'.

It was different from the agonizing sound of her own self breaking apart inside her.

――It was a terribly clear, pure sound, like a transparent wind brushing across the surface of water.

"Damn it."

A tiny bit of impurity mixed into that clear sound.

Drifting away from the crystal, at the edge of Clarissa's uncontrollable vision, a red-haired man passed by with a grimace on his profile. ――The clear sound had come from the man's hand.

A single flash of a drawn blade, a stroke of obsession reached at the end of relentless training—she realized. That man's single flash had taken Clarissa's head.

And the man who had taken her head sheathed the sword that had made that clear sound and muttered,

"She's still just a kid."

She could pretend she hadn't heard that noise.

Slowly, Clarissa's body collapsed, her rolling head no longer able to see the crystal her hand had held. But that was fine. She thought, maybe that was fine.

The sound of something breaking inside her had vanished.

If the last thing she heard was that clear, beautiful sound, she thought, it wasn't bad.

Because it had been a very, very clear, beautiful sound.

――Once the last ground dragon had crossed and it was confirmed no one remained, the magic was released, and the Milesta Dragonback Bridge brought its three-hundred-year history to an end.

The moment the deep violet glow faded and vanished, the anchored bridge girders tilted all at once, and a tremendous amount of stone plummeted into the valley. The delayed roar shook even the highway, and at the height and number of the rising plumes of water, Emilia narrowed her eyes in regret.

"I'm glad no one died, even if some people got hurt, but..."

Even that had been razor-thin; if a single thing had gone wrong, the outcome would have been different. And if there was a future that tilted for the worse, she thought there could have been a better future too.

One where the Dragonback Bridge, with its long history, hadn't had to collapse. ――Though Felt would probably say there was no use moping over what had already happened.

"I want a better result next time. So I can't just nod and accept it right away."

As Emilia made that resolve, she saw a crowd of people waving toward them on the cliff from a boat receding beyond the river mist. Waving back, Emilia was finally able to relax her long-tense fingers.

Among those gathered after crossing the bridge, voices rejoicing at their safety mingled with voices lamenting lost cargo. As mentioned earlier, dragon carriages had overturned in the bridge's shaking, leaving many injured. Filore was rushing around in a hurry.

Emilia also started to walk over to lend a hand, when――

"――Emilia-tan!"

"――――"

At that voice, Emilia gasped and turned around. Looking over, the castle floating in the gorge had brought its balcony close to the bank, and a boy leaning out as if he couldn't wait leaped across. Landing effortlessly, he ran straight toward her with Beatrice held in one arm.

"Lady Emilia, Ram will handle things here. Please go quiet that noisy Barusu."

"Ram... yes! Thank you!"

Told so by Ram, who was busy tending to the injured alongside Filore, Emilia nodded. Beyond Ram, Felt gave a thumbs-up, flashing a grin with her canine tooth.

She felt a little bad for Felt, who was still separated from Reinhard, but――

"Subaru...!"

Unable to wait any longer, Emilia closed the distance with the running Subaru. Soon, they both naturally slowed their pace, stopping just one step short of touching.

Facing him directly for the first time in so long—Subaru was just as Emilia knew him. In front of her, Subaru, holding Beatrice, scratched his cheek with one hand.

"Sorry I'm late, Emilia-tan. I wanted to show up in a ride flashier than a pumpkin carriage, but the castle kind of stalled."

"Sorry, I don't really get what you're saying."

"That's right, Subaru, in fact. Besides, I take offense at you making it sound like Mother's castle was at fault, I suppose. Without this castle, we couldn't have reached the bridge this fast, in fact."

"I know, I know! I'm sorry! Just hiding my embarrassment!"

Protested by Beatrice in his arms, Subaru's voice cracked in his usual way. Feeling relieved at the sight, Emilia tilted her head and looked at Beatrice.

"Beatrice, thank you reeally much too. Thanks to you, the bridge was saved."

"You should be grateful, I suppose. Once again, Subaru and Betty are completely drained, in fact."

"Kinda running on empty after back-to-back fights. Seriously glad we barely had enough."

"Mm, really. ...Really, yeah."

"Emilia-tan?"

Emilia nodded at the bashfully smiling Subaru and tried to continue, but for some reason, the words wouldn't come out right. Even Subaru looked puzzled.

"...Oh? I wonder why... I'm being weird, huh."

Crinkling the corners of his eyes in the smile Emilia knew so well, Subaru looked at her—and though she should have had so, so many things to say, she couldn't put them into words.

Somehow, it felt like her chest was completely full.

"Um, well, you see..."

"No need to rush, Emilia-tan. Not to brag, but after two months apart from you, I'm pretty dizzy from an Emiliase deficiency. That's not something you can easily replenish, so you'd better brace yourself for some sleepless nights listening to me ramble."

"I suppose. There was a limit to supplementing Emiliase with Beatromin, in fact."

"Hehe, really? Is that so? Then, yeah, maybe I had a Subaru deficiency too."

Not "maybe"—saying it aloud, she realized it was definitely true. While they were apart, of course she had missed everyone else too, but standing right in front of Subaru like this, she was certain. ――Emilia had been lonely without Subaru.

So――

"Let's take turns. Just like you want to talk, I have lots of things I want to talk about too. How about we trade off like that?"

"Yeah, I'd love nothing more. ...I'm really glad you're safe."

In that last, softly murmured line, she felt the raw, unguarded Subaru, and letting out a faint, raspy breath, Emilia gently reached out to his cheek. She felt like if she didn't catch him like that, Subaru would immediately hide that face away.

She always wanted Subaru to smile, yet knowing he had a face he tried not to show her made Emilia feel so――that was when it happened.

"――Ah."

The instant her fingertips touched Subaru's cheek. ――Deep in her chest, something wrenched tight.

Instinctively trying to grab Subaru's clothes, her hand instead clutched at her own chest. Unable even to draw breath to check what had happened, Emilia stared at Subaru in front of her.

"...Emilia?"

Seeing no pain or grief on Subaru's face, Emilia felt relieved. ――That meant she was the only one feeling this creaking ache deep in her chest right now.

"I'm――"

She tried to say "I'm okay," but she couldn't.

They had finally met again, and she didn't want him to make that face, yet a voiceless groan of pain slipped from Emilia's lips, and she collapsed to her knees.

Instantly, Subaru's arms shot out and caught her. ――At that moment, the creaking deep in her chest cried out with even greater heat, and at last, Emilia's consciousness was dyed white, pure white.

"Emilia?! Emilia! No way, Emilia――!!"

Subaru's voice sounded far away.

Even though he was right beside her, touching her, it was so, so far away, and lonely.

Happy to see him, yet so far away and lonely, tears spilled from Emilia's eyes.

By the time those spilled tears fell, Emilia's consciousness was no longer there.
